\chapter{Feature Tags, Mixins, and Utility
Concepts}\label{ch5-feature-tags-mixins-and-utility-concepts}

\section{The Feature Tag Idiom}\label{ch5-the-feature-tag-idiom}

Every non-trivial DSL built with MuDSL is assembled from a small set of
reusable units called \emph{feature tags}. A feature tag is a plain
struct, typically empty or near-empty at its outer level, that exposes a
single nested template:

\begin{code}
struct SomeFeature
{
  template <typename Derived> struct Mixin
  {
    // members injected into the user's DSL class
  };
};
\end{code}

The tag itself is what the user passes to
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}DSL}. The \texttt{Mixin} is what
gets inherited. This split exists so that the public name of a feature
(\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Pipeline},
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Operators}, and so on) stays
short and free of template parameters, while the actual injected members
remain parameterized on the derived DSL type. The two-level naming is
the load-bearing trick that lets features compose through multiple
inheritance without name clashes on the tag itself.

The mechanism by which mixins are joined to the user's class is the CRTP
base shown in Chapter 3. Its essential line is reproduced below:

\begin{code}
template <typename Derived, typename... Features>
struct DSL : Features::template Mixin<Derived>...
{
protected:
  constexpr Derived &self () noexcept { return static_cast<Derived &> (*this); }
  constexpr const Derived &self () const noexcept { return static_cast<const Derived &> (*this); }
};
\end{code}

\texttt{DSL} expands its \texttt{Features} pack by privately-or-publicly
inheriting each
\texttt{Feature:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Mixin\textless{}\allowbreak{}Derived\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
The result is that a class declared as
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}DSL\textless{}\allowbreak{}MyDSL,\allowbreak{} dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Pipeline,\allowbreak{} dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Operators\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
simultaneously derives from
\texttt{Pipeline:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Mixin\textless{}\allowbreak{}MyDSL\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
and
\texttt{Operators:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Mixin\textless{}\allowbreak{}MyDSL\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
Whatever members those mixins declare become reachable on \texttt{MyDSL}
itself, subject to access and overload resolution.

This chapter is the bridge between the CRTP foundation of Chapter 3 and
the individual feature chapters that follow. Each feature tag listed
here receives its own full treatment in a later chapter; the present
chapter catalogues the shared idiom, the three categories of feature,
the conditional-member contract, and the utility concepts that let user
code introspect which features a DSL has mixed in.

\section{Categories of Feature}\label{ch5-categories-of-feature}

Although every feature tag shares the same syntactic shape, their mixins
fall into three categories distinguished by what they contribute to the
derived class.

The first category is the \emph{pure marker} feature. Its \texttt{Mixin}
body is empty; the tag exists only so a DSL can advertise that it
``has'' the feature. The second is the \emph{method-injecting} feature,
whose \texttt{Mixin} adds one or more member functions ---
\texttt{wrap}, \texttt{make\_\allowbreak{}pred},
\texttt{make\_\allowbreak{}leaf}, \texttt{clear\_\allowbreak{}cache},
and so on. The third is the \emph{operator-injecting} feature, which
uses \texttt{friend} operators defined inside the mixin to wire up
arithmetic or piping syntax for the derived type.

These three categories are not enforced by the type system; they are a
descriptive taxonomy. A single feature tag could in principle mix all
three behaviors, although in practice the library keeps them separated.
Recognizing which category a given tag belongs to is the fastest way to
predict what writing it into a
\texttt{Features.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}} list will
actually do to the resulting DSL.

The sections below take each category in turn, with concrete examples
drawn verbatim from the header.

\section{Pure Marker Features}\label{ch5-pure-marker-features}

A pure marker feature has a \texttt{Mixin} whose body contributes
nothing. The \texttt{PatternMatch} tag is the clearest example:

\begin{code}
struct PatternMatch
{
  template <typename Derived> struct Mixin
  {
    // Feature presence marker; no additional methods needed since
    // dsl::match/when/otherwise are free functions in the dsl:: namespace.
  };
};
\end{code}

The comment in the source explains the rationale: the actual
functionality (\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}match},
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}when},
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}otherwise}) is delivered as free
functions in the \texttt{dsl} namespace, so the mixin has nothing to
inject. The tag still serves a purpose, because it lets a DSL declare
intent and lets generic code check for that declaration through the
\texttt{HasFeature} concept introduced later in this chapter.

Five further marker tags follow the same pattern. Each is a one-line
declaration of an empty \texttt{Mixin}:

\begin{code}
struct LazyFeature    { template <typename Derived> struct Mixin {}; };
struct Monadic        { template <typename Derived> struct Mixin {}; };
struct ResultFeature  { template <typename Derived> struct Mixin {}; };
struct CombinatorParser { template <typename Derived> struct Mixin {}; };
struct TaskPipeline   { template <typename Derived> struct Mixin {}; };
\end{code}

These correspond to integrations whose machinery lives elsewhere ---
\texttt{Lazy\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
(Chapter 19), the
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} type
and monadic helpers (Chapters 20 and 21),
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
(Chapter 22), parser combinators (Chapters 23 through 25), and
\texttt{Task}/\texttt{TaskChain} (Chapter 26). The tag on the DSL is
what lets a generic library function gate itself on the presence of that
integration, even when no methods are being injected.

A DSL can list any combination of these markers without paying any
runtime cost. Because each \texttt{Mixin} is empty, the resulting
derived class gains no data members and no virtual functions. The
inheritance is, in effect, a compile-time annotation. Markers matter
because they turn otherwise-invisible design intent into something the
type system can query, as shown in the concepts section below.

\section{Method-Injecting Features: Pipeline and
Operators}\label{ch5-method-injecting-features-pipeline-and-operators}

The \texttt{Pipeline} feature tag contributes a single static member,
\texttt{wrap}, which lifts a value into the pipeable domain:

\begin{code}
struct Pipeline
{
  template <typename Derived> struct Mixin
  {
    template <typename T>
    static constexpr T
    wrap (T &&v)
    {
      return std::forward<T> (v);
    }
  };
};
\end{code}

\texttt{wrap} is, mechanically, a perfect-forwarding identity. Its job
is not to transform the value but to provide a named entry point on the
DSL so that downstream \texttt{operator|} stages, documented in Chapter
6, can be chained. Because \texttt{wrap} is \texttt{static} and
\texttt{constexpr}, calling
\texttt{MyDSL:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}wrap(\allowbreak{}10)\allowbreak{}}
costs nothing at runtime and works in constant-evaluated contexts.

The \texttt{Operators} feature tag is structurally identical: one static
factory, \texttt{make\_\allowbreak{}pred}, which lifts a callable into
the library's composable \texttt{Predicate} type:

\begin{code}
struct Operators
{
  template <typename Derived> struct Mixin
  {
    template <typename F>
    static constexpr auto
    make_pred (F &&f)
    {
      return dsl::predicate (std::forward<F> (f));
    }
  };
};
\end{code}

Predicates built this way compose with \texttt{operator\&},
\texttt{operator|}, and \texttt{operator!} to form boolean expressions,
as Chapter 7 describes in detail. Note that the mixin does not store any
state of its own; it merely exposes a named constructor. This is the
common shape of a method-injecting feature: a thin, often static, member
function whose body forwards to a free function in the \texttt{dsl}
namespace.

The basic-usage example shipped with the library combines both features
and shows the typical call shape:

\begin{code}
struct BasicDSL : dsl::DSL<BasicDSL, dsl::Pipeline, dsl::Operators> {};

int main() {
  auto v = BasicDSL::wrap(5) | dsl::pipe([](int x) { return x + 3; })
           | dsl::pipe([](int x) { return x * 2; });
  auto gt5 = BasicDSL::make_pred([](int x) { return x > 5; });
  auto even = BasicDSL::make_pred([](int x) { return x % 2 == 0; });
  std::cout << v << " " << (gt5(v) && even(v)) << "\n";
}
\end{code}

Because both injected members are static, the DSL need not even be
instantiated to use them; the class name suffices. This is the idiomatic
shape for stateless features.

\section{Method-Injecting Features: AST, Rewrite,
CustomLiterals}\label{ch5-method-injecting-features-ast-rewrite-customliterals}

The \texttt{AST} feature tag contributes two static factories,
\texttt{make\_\allowbreak{}leaf} and \texttt{make\_\allowbreak{}node},
which are convenience wrappers around the free
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}leaf\textless{}\allowbreak{}Tag\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
and
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}node\textless{}\allowbreak{}Tag\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
functions covered in Chapter 11:

\begin{code}
struct AST
{
  template <typename Derived> struct Mixin
  {
    template <FixedString Tag, typename T>
    static ASTNode
    make_leaf (T &&val)
    {
      return dsl::leaf<Tag> (std::forward<T> (val));
    }

    template <FixedString Tag, typename... Children>
      requires (std::convertible_to<Children, ASTNode> && ...)
    static ASTNode
    make_node (Children &&...children)
    {
      return dsl::node<Tag> (std::forward<Children> (children)...);
    }
  };
};
\end{code}

Both factories take a \texttt{FixedString} non-type template parameter
(Chapter 4) naming the node tag. \texttt{make\_\allowbreak{}node}
carries a fold constraint requiring every child to be convertible to
\texttt{ASTNode}, which is the value type documented in Chapter 10. The
mixin is otherwise stateless; like \texttt{Pipeline} and
\texttt{Operators}, it only re-exports free functions under the DSL's
own name.

The \texttt{Rewrite} and \texttt{CustomLiterals} tags take a different
shape. Their mixins still inject methods, but the methods are guarded by
a \texttt{requires} clause that depends on the derived class. That
contract is important enough to deserve its own section, which follows
shortly.

\section{Operator-Injecting Features:
ExprTemplates}\label{ch5-operator-injecting-features-exprtemplates}

The third category uses \texttt{friend} operators defined inside the
mixin to wire up expression syntax for the derived type. The
\texttt{ExprTemplates} tag is the canonical example:

\begin{code}
struct ExprTemplates
{
  template <typename Derived> struct Mixin
  {
    template <typename R>
    friend constexpr auto
    operator+ (const Derived &lhs, const R &rhs)
    {
      return BinExpr<std::plus<>, Derived, R>{ lhs, rhs };
    }

    template <typename R>
    friend constexpr auto
    operator- (const Derived &lhs, const R &rhs)
    {
      return BinExpr<std::minus<>, Derived, R>{ lhs, rhs };
    }

    template <typename R>
    friend constexpr auto
    operator* (const Derived &lhs, const R &rhs)
    {
      return BinExpr<std::multiplies<>, Derived, R>{ lhs, rhs };
    }

    template <typename R>
    friend constexpr auto
    operator/ (const Derived &lhs, const R &rhs)
    {
      return BinExpr<std::divides<>, Derived, R>{ lhs, rhs };
    }

    friend constexpr auto
    operator- (const Derived &e)
    {
      return UnaryExpr<std::negate<>, Derived>{ e };
    }
  };
};
\end{code}

Each \texttt{friend} declaration is found through argument-dependent
lookup only when one operand has type \texttt{Derived}. The operators
return \texttt{BinExpr} or \texttt{UnaryExpr} expression-template nodes
(Chapters 15 and 16) rather than eagerly computing a value, which is
what makes the resulting expressions lazy.

The derived class is responsible for supplying an
\texttt{expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} member
that returns the underlying scalar, as the source comment notes. Without
that member, the eventual call to
\texttt{eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} on the built expression will
not compile. The \texttt{ExprLike} concept introduced later in this
chapter formalizes that expectation.

Because the operators are defined as friends \emph{inside the mixin},
they are injected into the enclosing namespace only when the mixin is
actually inherited. A DSL that does not list
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}ExprTemplates} in its feature
list will not get the operators, even if it otherwise looks like a
scalar wrapper. This is the precise mechanism by which operator
overloading is kept opt-in.

\section{The Conditional-Member
Contract}\label{ch5-the-conditional-member-contract}

Some mixin methods only make sense when the derived class provides a
particular static member. The library expresses this with a
\texttt{requires require\allowbreak{}s} clause on the method itself. The
\texttt{Rewrite} tag is the clearest example:

\begin{code}
struct Rewrite
{
  template <typename Derived> struct Mixin
  {
    ASTNode
    rewrite (const ASTNode &tree) const
      requires requires { Derived::rules; }
    {
      return Derived::rules.apply (tree);
    }
  };
};
\end{code}

The inner
\texttt{requires \{ Deriv\allowbreak{}ed:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}rules; \}}
expression checks that the derived class exposes a static member named
\texttt{rules}. Only then is \texttt{rewrite} part of the mixin's
overload set. A DSL that omits \texttt{rules} will not have a
\texttt{rewrite} method at all --- overload resolution will reject the
call rather than producing a hard error inside the template body.

The \texttt{CustomLiterals} tag uses the same pattern with
\texttt{Derived:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}literals}:

\begin{code}
struct CustomLiterals
{
  template <typename Derived> struct Mixin
  {
    static long double
    parse_literal (std::string_view input)
      requires requires { Derived::literals; }
    {
      return Derived::literals.parse (input);
    }
  };
};
\end{code}

In both cases the derived class must define a static member of the
appropriate type: \texttt{RewriteSet} for \texttt{rules},
\texttt{LiteralSet} for \texttt{literals}. The library does not
constrain the \emph{type} of that member through the concept, only its
existence and name; the well-formedness of
\texttt{rules.\allowbreak{}apply(\allowbreak{}tree)\allowbreak{}} or
\texttt{literals.\allowbreak{}parse(\allowbreak{}input)\allowbreak{}} is
checked separately when the method is actually invoked.

The mini-DSL example shipped with the library demonstrates the contract
for \texttt{CustomLiterals}:

\begin{code}
struct MiniDSL : dsl::DSL<MiniDSL, dsl::Pipeline, dsl::CustomLiterals> {
  static constexpr auto literals = dsl::literal_set(
      dsl::lit<"_km">([](long double v) { return v * 1000.0L; }),
      dsl::lit<"_m"> ([](long double v) { return v; }));
};

int main() {
  auto meters = MiniDSL::parse_literal("2.5_km");
  auto shown = MiniDSL::wrap(meters) | dsl::pipe([](long double x) { return x + 10.0L; });
  std::cout << shown << "\n";
}
\end{code}

The \texttt{static constexpr\allowbreak{} auto literals} declaration
satisfies the \texttt{requires} clause, which in turn makes
\texttt{parse\_\allowbreak{}literal} available as a static member of
\texttt{MiniDSL}. The equivalent pattern for \texttt{Rewrite} is a
\texttt{static inline au\allowbreak{}to rules = dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}rewrite\_\allowbreak{}set(\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{};}
declaration, as Chapter 13 documents.

This idiom is deliberately conservative. The constraint fires only on
the member name, not on its type, so users have latitude in how they
construct the rule set or literal set. The trade-off is that a
misspelled member name silently removes the method rather than producing
a type error at the point of declaration.

\section{Method-Injecting Features:
Memoization}\label{ch5-method-injecting-features-memoization}

The \texttt{Memoization} tag injects a single instance method,
\texttt{clear\_\allowbreak{}cache}, that delegates to a
\texttt{memo\_\allowbreak{}cache} member expected on the derived class:

\begin{code}
struct Memoization
{
  template <typename Derived> struct Mixin
  {
    void
    clear_cache ()
    {
      static_cast<Derived &> (*this).memo_cache.clear ();
    }
  };
};
\end{code}

Unlike \texttt{Pipeline:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}wrap} or
\texttt{Operators:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}make\_\allowbreak{}pred},
\texttt{clear\_\allowbreak{}cache} is non-static and uses the CRTP
downcast to reach the derived object. The expected
\texttt{memo\_\allowbreak{}cache} member is not constrained by a
\texttt{requires} clause here; the body simply assumes it. Chapter 18
covers the \texttt{memoize} helper and the
\texttt{MemoizedCallable\allowbreak{}} wrapper that typically produces
such a cache.

This tag illustrates a subtle point about the method-injecting category:
not every injected method is static. Instance methods that downcast to
\texttt{Derived} are equally valid, and they are the natural shape when
the injected behavior needs to mutate state held by the derived class
itself.

\section{Forward Declarations and Definition
Order}\label{ch5-forward-declarations-and-definition-order}

The header forward-declares every feature tag before any of them is
defined. This is what allows the order of definitions in the header to
differ from the order in which they are used: a mixin may reference
another feature's machinery, or be referenced by it, without forcing a
strict topological ordering on the file.

For the user, the practical consequence is that the entire feature-tag
vocabulary is available as soon as \texttt{MuDSL.\allowbreak{}hpp} is
included. There is no need to include sub-headers or to declare tags in
any particular sequence. The following are all usable in any order in
user code:

\begin{code}
struct A : dsl::DSL<A, dsl::Pipeline, dsl::Rewrite>          { static inline auto rules = dsl::rewrite_set(); };
struct B : dsl::DSL<B, dsl::CustomLiterals, dsl::ExprTemplates> {
  static constexpr auto literals = dsl::literal_set();
  double expr_value() const { return 0.0; }
};
struct C : dsl::DSL<C, dsl::AST, dsl::PatternMatch, dsl::Memoization> {};
\end{code}

The forward declarations are an implementation detail, but they shape
how user code may be written. Because the tags are complete types by the
time \texttt{DSL} is instantiated, mixins may freely refer to sibling
tags in their own bodies, and users may freely order their feature lists
however reads most naturally.

\section{How Features Compose}\label{ch5-how-features-compose}

A DSL listing several feature tags gains the union of their mixins. The
mechanism is the simple variadic expansion in the \texttt{DSL} base:

\begin{code}
template <typename Derived, typename... Features>
struct DSL : Features::template Mixin<Derived>...
{ /* ... */ };
\end{code}

Each listed tag contributes its
\texttt{Mixin\textless{}\allowbreak{}Derived\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
as one base class of \texttt{DSL}, and \texttt{DSL} itself is then a
base of the user's class. The result is linear multiple inheritance over
a handful of empty or near-empty bases, which modern compilers collapse
to nothing in the common case.

A four-feature DSL looks like this in practice:

\begin{code}
struct RichDSL
  : dsl::DSL<RichDSL,
             dsl::Pipeline,
             dsl::Operators,
             dsl::AST,
             dsl::Rewrite>
{
  static inline auto rules = dsl::rewrite_set(
      /* ... rules ... */);
};

int main() {
  auto tree = RichDSL::make_node<"root">(RichDSL::make_leaf<"n">(42));
  RichDSL r;
  auto optimized = r.rewrite(tree);
  auto pred = RichDSL::make_pred([](int x) { return x > 0; });
  (void)optimized; (void)pred;
}
\end{code}

Here \texttt{RichDSL} simultaneously has \texttt{wrap} (from
\texttt{Pipeline}), \texttt{make\_\allowbreak{}pred} (from
\texttt{Operators}),
\texttt{make\_\allowbreak{}leaf}/\texttt{make\_\allowbreak{}node} (from
\texttt{AST}), and \texttt{rewrite} (from \texttt{Rewrite}, gated on the
\texttt{rules} member). The four features coexist without conflict
because each contributes members under distinct names.

Composition is not free of caveats. Two features that inject
identically-named members would produce an ambiguity at the derived
class, which is why the library's feature set is designed with disjoint
member names. Users writing their own mixins, as the next section
illustrates, should follow the same convention.

\section{Building a Feature Tag: An Illustrative
Example}\label{ch5-building-a-feature-tag-an-illustrative-example}

The feature-tag pattern is intentionally simple, and the clearest way to
demystify it is to write one. The code below is illustrative user code,
not part of the library; it shows the minimum scaffolding needed to add
a custom feature to a DSL.

\begin{code}
// User code — not part of MuDSL.
namespace dsl {
struct Greeter
{
  template <typename Derived> struct Mixin
  {
    template <typename T>
    static constexpr std::string
    greet (const T &who)
    {
      return "hello, " + std::string{who};
    }
  };
};
} // namespace dsl

struct GreetDSL : dsl::DSL<GreetDSL, dsl::Greeter> {};

static_assert(GreetDSL::greet("world") == "hello, world");
\end{code}

The tag is an empty struct. The \texttt{Mixin} is a class template with
a single type parameter, conventionally named \texttt{Derived}. Members
declared inside the mixin become members of any class that inherits
\texttt{Greeter:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Mixin\textless{}\allowbreak{}Derived\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
through the \texttt{DSL} base.

A slightly richer version can demonstrate the conditional-member
contract:

\begin{code}
// User code — not part of MuDSL.
namespace dsl {
struct VerboseGreeter
{
  template <typename Derived> struct Mixin
  {
    static std::string
    formal_greet (std::string_view who)
      requires requires { Derived::prefix; }
    {
      return std::string{Derived::prefix} + ", " + std::string{who};
    }
  };
};
} // namespace dsl

struct FormalDSL
  : dsl::DSL<FormalDSL, dsl::VerboseGreeter>
{
  static constexpr std::string_view prefix = "greetings";
};

static_assert(FormalDSL::formal_greet("stranger") == "greetings, stranger");
\end{code}

This mirrors the shape of \texttt{Rewrite} and \texttt{CustomLiterals}
exactly: a method on the mixin, guarded by a
\texttt{requires require\allowbreak{}s} clause naming a static member
that the derived class must supply. The pattern is compositional
precisely because it is so small; a custom feature is just another
struct with a nested \texttt{Mixin} template.

\section{The Utility Concepts}\label{ch5-the-utility-concepts}

The header provides five concepts that introspect which features a type
has mixed in. They are defined verbatim as follows:

\begin{code}
template <typename T, typename F>
concept HasFeature = std::derived_from<T, typename F::template Mixin<T>>;

template <typename T>
concept Pipeable = HasFeature<T, Pipeline>;

template <typename T>
concept ExprLike = HasFeature<T, ExprTemplates>
                   && requires (const T &t) { t.expr_value (); };

template <typename T>
concept HasLiterals
    = HasFeature<T, CustomLiterals> && requires { T::literals; };

template <typename T>
concept Rewritable = HasFeature<T, Rewrite> && requires { T::rules; };
\end{code}

\texttt{HasFeature} is the foundation. It checks that \texttt{T} derives
from
\texttt{F:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Mixin\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
--- exactly the relationship that the \texttt{DSL} base establishes. The
four remaining concepts layer additional \texttt{requires} clauses on
top: \texttt{Pipeable} is
\texttt{HasFeature\textless{}\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{} Pipeline\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
alone, while \texttt{ExprLike}, \texttt{HasLiterals}, and
\texttt{Rewritable} add a check for the member that the corresponding
feature needs to function.

The reason \texttt{ExprLike} also requires
\texttt{t.\allowbreak{}expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
is that the \texttt{ExprTemplates} mixin alone does not supply that
member; it only injects operators. The derived class must independently
provide \texttt{expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
for the operators to eventually evaluate. Folding that requirement into
the concept lets generic code treat any \texttt{ExprLike} type as a
fully usable expression-template leaf without further checks.

\texttt{HasLiterals} and \texttt{Rewritable} fold in the same static
member that the conditional-member contract checks for. This means the
concepts and the \texttt{requires} clauses on the mixin methods are
aligned: a type that satisfies \texttt{Rewritable} is guaranteed to have
a callable \texttt{rewrite}, and a type that satisfies
\texttt{HasLiterals} is guaranteed to have a callable
\texttt{parse\_\allowbreak{}literal}.

\section{Using the Concepts in Generic
Code}\label{ch5-using-the-concepts-in-generic-code}

The concepts are intended for use in user-written templates. The most
common shape is a function template constrained on a single feature
concept, so that the function body can rely on the feature's members
being present.

A function constrained with \texttt{Pipeable} can assume that
\texttt{wrap} is available:

\begin{code}
template <typename D>
  requires dsl::Pipeable<D>
auto
double_then_inc (int x)
{
  return D::wrap(x)
       | dsl::pipe([](int v) { return v * 2; })
       | dsl::pipe([](int v) { return v + 1; });
}
\end{code}

A function constrained with \texttt{ExprLike} can assume both the
operators and \texttt{expr\_\allowbreak{}value}:

\begin{code}
template <typename D>
  requires dsl::ExprLike<D>
auto
polynomial (const D &a, const D &b)
{
  return (a + b) * a - b;   // lazy BinExpr tree
}
\end{code}

A function constrained with \texttt{HasLiterals} can call
\texttt{parse\_\allowbreak{}literal}:

\begin{code}
template <typename D>
  requires dsl::HasLiterals<D>
long double
parse_with (std::string_view s)
{
  return D::parse_literal(s);
}
\end{code}

A function constrained with \texttt{Rewritable} can call
\texttt{rewrite} on an instance:

\begin{code}
template <typename D>
  requires dsl::Rewritable<D>
dsl::ASTNode
optimize (const D &dsl, const dsl::ASTNode &tree)
{
  return dsl.rewrite(tree);
}
\end{code}

The generic \texttt{HasFeature} concept lets user code write
feature-agnostic helpers, including over its own custom tags. The
illustrative \texttt{Greeter} tag from earlier can be checked the same
way:

\begin{code}
template <typename D>
  requires dsl::HasFeature<D, dsl::Greeter>   // user tag, library concept
std::string
say_hi (std::string_view who)
{
  return D::greet(who);
}
\end{code}

This is the mechanism by which marker features earn their keep. A DSL
that lists \texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Monadic} advertises
that it participates in monadic combinators, even though the tag injects
no methods; user code can then write a template constrained with
\texttt{HasFeature\textless{}\allowbreak{}D,\allowbreak{} dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Monadic\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
to gate behavior on that advertisement.

\section{Why Marker Features Still
Matter}\label{ch5-why-marker-features-still-matter}

It is tempting to dismiss an empty \texttt{Mixin} as ceremony. The
markers earn their place through the concepts. Without a tag on the DSL,
there is no
\texttt{Mixin\textless{}\allowbreak{}Derived\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
base, and therefore no way for \texttt{HasFeature} to return true. The
marker is the surface that the concept queries.

Consider a generic combinator that should only accept DSLs participating
in the result-based error-flow integration. With the
\texttt{ResultFeature} marker in place, the combinator's signature is
straightforward:

\begin{code}
template <typename D>
  requires dsl::HasFeature<D, dsl::ResultFeature>
auto
run_with_care (D &dsl, /* ... */)
{
  // body may assume the DSL opts into Result<T,E> flow
}
\end{code}

The body of the function need not call any injected method, because
there is no injected method to call. The constraint is purely
documentary at the type level --- but it is enforced documentary, which
is exactly what a static-typing toolkit should provide.

The same reasoning applies to \texttt{LazyFeature}, \texttt{Monadic},
\texttt{CombinatorParser\allowbreak{}}, and \texttt{TaskPipeline}. Each
marker is a contract that the DSL author has elected to advertise, and
each can be checked by user code through \texttt{HasFeature}. The later
chapters devoted to those integrations (Chapters 19 through 26) assume
that the corresponding marker has been listed where appropriate.

